is to control the game.\n\nThe 77 play is the safest and most optimal play. Then I double-check to make sure. Okay, I have ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 and the valid move is "pair: ♠7 ♣7". I must select that exact pair.\n\nThe strategy is set: beat the low pair with 77. The goal is to set up a path to deploy that monster straight and AAA trio. This is the calculated and optimal move to both block the Landlord and set up a winning hand.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My hand contains a powerful 10-card straight (4-5-6-7-8-9-10-J-Q-K) and a strong triplet of Aces (AAA). To preserve the straight, I must retain at least one card of each rank from 4 to K. I have two 6s, two 8s, but three 7s. Playing the pair of 6s or 8s would leave me with zero of those ranks, destroying the straight. Playing the pair of 7s leaves one 7 remaining, keeping the straight intact.\n\nReason: The Landlord played a pair of 3s, which is the lowest possible pair. Playing the pair of 7s is an efficient response that beats the current hand without wasting high cards like Aces. Crucially, this move allows me to discard "extra" cards while maintaining the structure of my winning hand (the 10-card straight and AAA). Passing would leave me with these extra 7s to deal with later, and playing AA is an unnecessary overcommitment this early.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠7", "♣7"]}'}
